    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        EyeMulator: Improving Code Language Models by Mimicking Human Visual <span class="search-hit mathjax">Attention</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+Y">Yifan Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Huang%2C+C">Chen Huang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+Y">Yueke Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+J">Jiahao Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+T+J">Toby Jia-Jun Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=McMillan%2C+C">Collin McMillan</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Leach%2C+K">Kevin Leach</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Huang%2C+Y">Yu Huang</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2508.16771v3-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Code <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2508.16771v3-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2508.16771v3-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2508.16771v3-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Code <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span> (CodeLLMs) learn token importance from data correlations, whereas human developers attend selectively to semantically salient code. We present EyeMulator, a <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span>-agnostic method that injects human visual-attention priors into CodeLLM fine-tuning without architectural changes. EyeMulator distills eye-tracking data into semantic salience and gaze-transition priors, then uses them to reweight token-level training losses. Across six backbones, two data regimes, and three CodeXGLUE tasks, the reported configurations yield positive matched-metric deltas in all 36 <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span>-task-setting cells. Effects are largest for structure-preserving completion and translation, while summarization shows smaller but positive METEOR deltas. Session-mode and component-ablation analyses further show that reading, writing, semantic, and transition-derived priors provide complementary signal. Human-attention artifacts are available at https://zenodo.org/records/17205682.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2508.16771v3-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2508.16771v3-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 2 July, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 22 August, 2025;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> August 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
