    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Beyond Individuals: Collective Predictive Coding for Memory, <span class="search-hit mathjax">Attention</span>, and the Emergence of Language
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Taniguchi%2C+T">Tadahiro Taniguchi</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2508.15859v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;individual cognitive systems. From the perspective of the Collective Predictive Coding (CPC) hypothesis -- a framework for understanding these faculties and the emergence of <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> at the group level -- we introduce a hypothetical idea: that&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2508.15859v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2508.15859v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2508.15859v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        This commentary extends the discussion by Parr et al. on memory and attention beyond individual cognitive systems. From the perspective of the Collective Predictive Coding (CPC) hypothesis -- a framework for understanding these faculties and the emergence of <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> at the group level -- we introduce a hypothetical idea: that <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>, with its embedded distributional semantics, serves as a collectively formed external representation. CPC generalises the concepts of individual memory and attention to the collective level. This offers a new perspective on how shared linguistic structures, which may embrace collective world <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> learned through next-word prediction, emerge from and shape group-level cognition.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2508.15859v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2508.15859v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

